### What is offset pagination?

Offset pagination uses LIMIT and OFFSET to skip a fixed number of rows and return the next page. In JPA, this is `setFirstResult(offset)` and `setMaxResults(pageSize)`. It is simple to implement and supports jumping to any page number, but the cost grows linearly with the offset because the database must scan and discard all skipped rows.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Transaction;
import jakarta.persistence.EntityManager;
import jakarta.persistence.PersistenceContext;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.util.List;

@Service
public class OffsetPaginationService {

    @PersistenceContext
    private EntityManager em;

    @Transactional(readOnly = true)
    public List<Transaction> getStatementPage(
            String accountId, int page, int size) {
        return em.createQuery(
            "SELECT t FROM Transaction t "
          + "WHERE t.accountId = :acct "
          + "ORDER BY t.createdAt DESC, t.id DESC",
            Transaction.class
        ).setParameter("acct", accountId)
         .setFirstResult(page * size)  // 0-based offset
         .setMaxResults(size)
         .getResultList();
    }
}
```

```text
Page 0 (first page):
  SQL: SELECT * FROM transactions
       WHERE account_id = 'SAV2001'
       ORDER BY created_at DESC, id DESC
       LIMIT 20 OFFSET 0
  Cost: scan 20 rows. Fast.

Page 90000 (deep page):
  SQL: SELECT * FROM transactions
       WHERE account_id = 'SAV2001'
       ORDER BY created_at DESC, id DESC
       LIMIT 20 OFFSET 1800000
  Cost: scan 1,800,000 rows, discard 1,799,980, return 20.
  Slow! Time grows linearly with offset.
```

### What is keyset (cursor/seek) pagination?

Keyset pagination remembers the sort key of the last row returned and uses a WHERE predicate to seek directly to the next page. With an index on the sort columns, this is an index seek with constant cost regardless of how deep you are in the result set.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Transaction;
import jakarta.persistence.EntityManager;
import jakarta.persistence.PersistenceContext;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.time.Instant;
import java.util.List;

@Service
public class KeysetPaginationService {

    @PersistenceContext
    private EntityManager em;

    @Transactional(readOnly = true)
    public List<Transaction> getNextPage(
            String accountId, Instant lastCreatedAt,
            Long lastId, int size) {

        if (lastCreatedAt == null) {
            // First page: no cursor yet
            return em.createQuery(
                "SELECT t FROM Transaction t "
              + "WHERE t.accountId = :acct "
              + "ORDER BY t.createdAt DESC, t.id DESC",
                Transaction.class
            ).setParameter("acct", accountId)
             .setMaxResults(size)
             .getResultList();
        }

        // Subsequent pages: seek from last seen key
        return em.createQuery(
            "SELECT t FROM Transaction t "
          + "WHERE t.accountId = :acct "
          + "AND (t.createdAt < :lastTs "
          + "     OR (t.createdAt = :lastTs AND t.id < :lastId)) "
          + "ORDER BY t.createdAt DESC, t.id DESC",
            Transaction.class
        ).setParameter("acct", accountId)
         .setParameter("lastTs", lastCreatedAt)
         .setParameter("lastId", lastId)
         .setMaxResults(size)
         .getResultList();
    }
}
```

```text
First page:
  SQL: SELECT * FROM transactions
       WHERE account_id = 'SAV2001'
       ORDER BY created_at DESC, id DESC
       LIMIT 20
  Returns 20 rows. Last row: created_at='2024-01-10', id=54321

Next page (cursor = '2024-01-10', 54321):
  SQL: SELECT * FROM transactions
       WHERE account_id = 'SAV2001'
       AND (created_at < '2024-01-10'
            OR (created_at = '2024-01-10' AND id < 54321))
       ORDER BY created_at DESC, id DESC
       LIMIT 20
  Index seek: jumps directly to the right position.
  Cost: constant, regardless of how many rows came before.
```

### How is offset pagination different from keyset pagination?

| Aspect | Offset Pagination | Keyset Pagination |
|---|---|---|
| SQL mechanism | LIMIT + OFFSET | WHERE key > :lastKey LIMIT |
| Cost of deep pages | O(offset) -- scans all skipped rows | O(1) -- index seek to last key |
| Random page access | Yes (jump to page 500) | No (forward/backward only) |
| Stability under inserts | Unstable (rows shift) | Stable (anchors on value) |
| Implementation complexity | Simple | Moderate (composite cursor) |
| UI pattern | Page numbers (1, 2, 3, ...) | Infinite scroll, Load More |
| Index requirement | Optional (helps performance) | Required on sort columns |

```java
// Offset: simple but slow at depth
// Good for: admin dashboards, small result sets, page-number UIs
// Bad for: million-row tables, deep scrolling

// Keyset: fast at any depth
// Good for: infinite scroll, mobile statements, large exports
// Bad for: "jump to page 500" UIs (no random access)
```

### When does OFFSET kill performance?

OFFSET kills performance on large tables with deep paging. The database must physically scan and discard every row before the offset, even if only 10 rows are returned. This is a common production incident with report tools and export features.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Transaction;
import jakarta.persistence.EntityManager;
import jakarta.persistence.PersistenceContext;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.util.List;

@Service
public class OffsetPerformanceDemo {

    @PersistenceContext
    private EntityManager em;

    @Transactional(readOnly = true)
    public List<Transaction> getDeepPage() {
        return em.createQuery(
            "SELECT t FROM Transaction t ORDER BY t.createdAt DESC",
            Transaction.class
        ).setFirstResult(5_000_000)
         .setMaxResults(20)
         .getResultList();
        // DB scans 5,000,000 rows, discards 4,999,980, returns 20.
        // On a 10M row table: may take 30+ seconds!
    }
}
```

```text
Performance comparison on 10 million rows:

  Offset page 1 (OFFSET 0):         ~2 ms
  Offset page 1000 (OFFSET 20000):   ~50 ms
  Offset page 50000 (OFFSET 1000000): ~2 seconds
  Offset page 250000 (OFFSET 5000000): ~15 seconds
  Offset page 500000 (OFFSET 10000000): ~30 seconds (timeout!)

  Keyset at any depth: ~2 ms (constant)
```

### What about position instability with offset?

Offset pagination anchors on row position, not row values. If new rows are inserted between two page requests, the same row can appear on two consecutive pages, or a row can be skipped entirely.

```text
Scenario: Priya views page 2 of her statement (20 per page)
  Page 1: rows 1-20
  Between page 1 and page 2 requests:
    New transaction inserted at position 5
  Page 2: OFFSET 20, LIMIT 20
    Row 20 is now at position 21
    Row 20 appears on BOTH page 1 and page 2! (duplicate)
    Row 21 shifts to position 22 and is skipped.

Keyset pagination does not have this problem:
  Page 1 returns transactions with id > X
  Page 2 asks: WHERE id < lastSeenId
  New inserts with id > lastSeenId do not affect page 2.
  No duplicates, no skips.
```

### What are common mistakes with pagination?

The most common mistakes involve missing tiebreakers, using offset on large tables, and paginating over JOIN FETCH queries.

```java
// WRONG: no tiebreaker in ORDER BY
"SELECT t FROM Transaction t ORDER BY t.createdAt DESC"
// Two transactions at the same timestamp?
// Ordering is non-deterministic. Same row may appear on two pages.

// CORRECT: add a unique tiebreaker
"SELECT t FROM Transaction t ORDER BY t.createdAt DESC, t.id DESC"
// Even if createdAt is identical, id ensures deterministic order.
```

```java
// WRONG: offset pagination on a 50M row ledger
query.setFirstResult(49_000_000).setMaxResults(20);
// Scans 49 million rows. Timeout!

// CORRECT: keyset pagination for deep scrolling
"WHERE createdAt < :lastTs OR (createdAt = :lastTs AND id < :lastId)"
// Index seek: constant time regardless of depth.
```

```java
// WRONG: paginating a JOIN FETCH query
"SELECT a FROM Account a JOIN FETCH a.transactions"
// Page<Account> with Pageable
// JOIN multiplies rows. Hibernate paginates IN MEMORY.
// WARNING: HHH000104: firstResult/maxResults with collection fetch
// Loads ALL rows into memory, then paginates. OutOfMemoryError!

// CORRECT: paginate root entity, then fetch collections
"SELECT a FROM Account a WHERE a.status = 'ACTIVE'"
// Paginate accounts first, then batch-fetch transactions
```

### How does Spring Data support keyset pagination?

Spring Data (since version 3.1) supports keyset scrolling through the `ScrollPosition` and `Window` APIs. For older versions, use custom queries with cursor parameters.

```java
package com.bank.repository;

import com.bank.model.Transaction;
import org.springframework.data.domain.ScrollPosition;
import org.springframework.data.domain.Sort;
import org.springframework.data.domain.Window;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;

public interface TransactionRepository
        extends JpaRepository<Transaction, Long> {

    // Spring Data 3.1+ keyset scrolling
    Window<Transaction> findByAccountId(
        String accountId,
        ScrollPosition position,
        Sort sort
    );
}
```

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Transaction;
import com.bank.repository.TransactionRepository;
import org.springframework.data.domain.ScrollPosition;
import org.springframework.data.domain.Sort;
import org.springframework.data.domain.Window;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class KeysetScrollService {

    private final TransactionRepository txnRepo;

    public KeysetScrollService(TransactionRepository txnRepo) {
        this.txnRepo = txnRepo;
    }

    public Window<Transaction> firstPage(String accountId) {
        return txnRepo.findByAccountId(
            accountId,
            ScrollPosition.keyset(),  // start from beginning
            Sort.by(Sort.Direction.DESC, "createdAt", "id")
        );
    }

    public Window<Transaction> nextPage(
            String accountId, Window<Transaction> current) {
        return txnRepo.findByAccountId(
            accountId,
            current.positionAt(current.size() - 1),  // cursor
            Sort.by(Sort.Direction.DESC, "createdAt", "id")
        );
    }
}
```

### What about indexing for pagination?

Both pagination strategies benefit from proper indexes, but keyset pagination absolutely requires one. The index must cover the sort columns plus the WHERE clause filter.

```text
Recommended index for keyset pagination:

CREATE INDEX idx_txn_keyset
  ON transactions (account_id, created_at DESC, id DESC);

This index supports:
1. WHERE account_id = ? (equality filter)
2. ORDER BY created_at DESC, id DESC (sort)
3. Keyset seek: AND (created_at < ? OR (created_at = ? AND id < ?))

Without this index:
  Keyset pagination falls back to sequential scan.
  Performance advantage over offset disappears.

With this index:
  Both first page and page 500,000 return in ~2ms.
```
